@node Purpose
@chapter Purpose

This library provides MSVC symbol demangling functionality, currently
using the existing LLVM's MSVC demangler code.  It can be used by BFD,
GDB and binutils to demangle Microsoft Visual C++ symbols.

@node Licensing
@chapter Licensing

This library is licensed under Apache 2.0 with LLVM Exception.

@node API
@chapter API

The library exposes a C API compatible with libiberty's demangling
interface.

@section Core Demangling Functions

@deftypefun {char *} msvc_demangle (const char *@var{mangled}, int @var{options})
Demangle an MSVC symbol name.  Returns the demangled string, or
@code{NULL} if demangling fails.  Takes the same options as libiberty's
@code{cplus_demangle} for compatibility.  The caller must free the
returned string.
@end deftypefun

@section MSVC-Specific Utility Functions

The MSVC demangler does not provide an equivalent of libiberty's
@code{cplus_demangle_v3_components}, which returns a structured AST
from a mangled name.  Implementing one would require converting the
LLVM demangler's AST into libiberty's @code{demangle_component} tree.
Instead, an implementation is provided for the queries GDB would
derive from libiberty's AST: @code{cp_class_name_from_physname},
@code{method_name_from_physname} (defined in @file{gdb/cp-support.c})
and the constructor/destructor tests.  They are reached through the
libiberty entry points listed in the libiberty Registration section.

@deftypefun {char *} llvm_msvc_class_name_from_physname (const char *@var{physname})
Extract the qualified enclosing class name from an MSVC method
physname.  Returns a malloc'd string (caller frees), or @code{NULL} if
@var{physname} is not a recognised MSVC method physname.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} llvm_msvc_method_name_from_physname (const char *@var{physname})
Extract the bare (unqualified) method name from an MSVC method
physname.  Returns a malloc'd string (caller frees), or @code{NULL} if
@var{physname} is not a recognised MSVC method physname.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun void llvm_msvc_structor_kind (const char *@var{mangled}, enum gnu_v3_ctor_kinds *@var{ctor_kind}, enum gnu_v3_dtor_kinds *@var{dtor_kind})
The MSVC counterpart of libiberty's @code{is_ctor_or_dtor}.  Both
results are 0 unless @var{mangled} is a function whose name is
@samp{??0} (complete-object constructor, including templated
constructors @samp{??$?0}), @samp{??1} (base-object destructor),
@samp{??_D} (complete-object destructor of a class with virtual
bases), or @samp{??_G} / @samp{??_E} (scalar / vector deleting
destructor).
@end deftypefun

@section BFD Integration Functions

@deftypefun {char *} bfd_demangle (bfd *@var{abfd}, const char *@var{mangled}, int @var{options})
Unified BFD demangling function.  Automatically detects symbol type
and uses the appropriate demangler.

@table @var
@item abfd
BFD object context.
@item mangled
The mangled symbol name.
@item options
Demangling options (@code{DMGL_*} flags).
@end table

Returns the demangled string (caller must free), or @code{NULL} if
demangling failed.
@end deftypefun

@section libiberty Registration

@deftypefun void cplus_demangle_set_msvc_ops (const struct msvc_demangler_ops *@var{ops})
Register the MSVC demangler's callbacks with libiberty.  Declared in
@file{include/demangle.h}; libdemangle-msvc provides the table
@code{msvc_demangler_ops}.  Its members are @code{mangled_start},
@code{demangle}, @code{ctor_kind}, @code{dtor_kind},
@code{class_name_from_physname} and @code{method_name_from_physname}.
Once registered, @code{cplus_demangle} asks @code{mangled_start} about
every name and dispatches those it returns unchanged to
@code{demangle} whenever the current style is @code{auto_demangling}
or @code{msvc_demangling}.  Passing
@code{NULL} disables MSVC demangling.  This entry point is called from
@code{bfd_init} when BFD is built with @code{HAVE_MSVC_DEMANGLER}
defined.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {const char *} cplus_demangle_msvc_mangled_start (const char *@var{name})
The registered @code{mangled_start}: where the MSVC-mangled part of
@var{name} starts, or @code{NULL} (also when no table is registered).
@code{bfd_demangle} and @command{c++filt} call it for every name; text
before the returned position is printed in front of the demangled part,
and @samp{@@} inside an MSVC name is not treated as a version or
@samp{@@plt} suffix.
@end deftypefun

libiberty forwards the remaining callbacks, again only for names
@code{mangled_start} returns unchanged, and returns 0 or @code{NULL}
when no table is registered:

@deftypefun {enum gnu_v3_ctor_kinds} is_msvc_mangled_ctor (const char *@var{name})
@deftypefunx {enum gnu_v3_dtor_kinds} is_msvc_mangled_dtor (const char *@var{name})
MSVC counterparts of @code{is_gnu_v3_mangled_ctor} and
@code{is_gnu_v3_mangled_dtor}.
@end deftypefun

@deftypefun {char *} cplus_demangle_msvc_class_name (const char *@var{physname})
@deftypefunx {char *} cplus_demangle_msvc_method_name (const char *@var{physname})
Used by GDB's @code{cp_class_name_from_physname} and
@code{method_name_from_physname} before their AST walk.
@end deftypefun

The MSVC style is also exposed in libiberty's
@code{libiberty_demanglers[]} table as @samp{msvc}, so:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@command{c++filt -s msvc} / @command{c++filt --format=msvc} selects
the MSVC style explicitly.
@item
@command{(gdb) set demangle-style msvc} does the same in GDB.
@end itemize

When no handler is registered, an explicit MSVC style still parses,
but @code{cplus_demangle} returns @code{NULL} for every input.

@node Integration
@chapter Integration

The library is built with libtool as @code{libdemangle-msvc}, the way
@code{libsframe} is.  When it is enabled, @code{bfd_init} refers to
it, so @code{libbfd} links it, and it is installed along with
@code{libbfd}.  A shared @code{libbfd} depends on the shared
@code{libdemangle-msvc}; a program that links the static @code{libbfd}
links @code{libdemangle-msvc} as well.  It is a C++ library, so GDB and
every program that links @code{libbfd} (binutils, gas, ld, gprof,
gprofng and the simulators) are linked with the C++ compiler.
@code{libiberty} remains free of any dependency on it.

Flow of control for a tool such as @command{c++filt} or @command{gdb}:

@enumerate
@item
The tool calls @code{bfd_init} during start-up.
@item
When @code{libbfd} was configured with @code{HAVE_MSVC_DEMANGLER},
@code{bfd_init} calls @code{cplus_demangle_set_msvc_ops
(&msvc_demangler_ops)}, installing the MSVC callbacks into libiberty.
@item
The tool calls @code{bfd_demangle} (or @code{cplus_demangle} directly).
@item
@code{bfd_demangle} asks @code{cplus_demangle_msvc_mangled_start}
where the mangled part starts, keeps any text before it aside and
forwards the rest to @code{cplus_demangle}.
@item
@code{cplus_demangle} dispatches to the registered MSVC handler when
the style is @code{auto}/@code{msvc} and @code{mangled_start} returns
the symbol unchanged; otherwise it falls through to the Itanium / Rust /
D / Ada demanglers.
@end enumerate

If a tool was not linked against @code{libdemangle-msvc}, no handler
is registered and @code{cplus_demangle} simply returns @code{NULL} for
MSVC-shaped names.

@section Symbol Format Detection

The single detection function is @code{msvc_mangled_start}, registered
as @code{mangled_start}.  It returns @var{name} itself for names whose
first character is @samp{?}, which no other supported scheme (Itanium,
Rust legacy, D, GNAT, Java) produces, and for the exception-handling
throw-info names @samp{_CT??_R0@dots{}}, @samp{_CTA@var{n}@dots{}} and
@samp{_TI[C][V][U]@var{n}@dots{}}.  A throw-info name is a
concatenation that LLVM's parser does not accept as a whole, so each
mangled part is demangled separately: @samp{_CTA2?AVruntime_error@@std@@@@}
becomes @samp{_CTA2 std::runtime_error}.

MSVC EH and unwind tables are named @samp{$@var{tag}$?@var{mangled}},
for example @samp{$cppxdata$?f@@@@YAXXZ},
@samp{$handlerMap$0$?f@@@@YAXXZ} or @samp{$unwind$?f@@@@YAXXZ}.
For these @code{msvc_mangled_start} returns the position of the
@samp{?}; @code{bfd_demangle} and @command{c++filt} demangle from
there and put the tag back, giving @samp{$cppxdata$f(void)}, so a table
is never shown under its function's name.

@section Registration Architecture

To keep @code{libdemangle-msvc} out of @code{libiberty}, the MSVC
callbacks are registered at run-time through libiberty's
@code{cplus_demangle_set_msvc_ops}:

@itemize @bullet
@item
When MSVC demangling is disabled, nothing links
@code{libdemangle-msvc} and no handler is installed.
@item
When it is enabled, @code{bfd_init} calls
@code{cplus_demangle_set_msvc_ops(&msvc_demangler_ops)}.
@item
@code{cplus_demangle} then dispatches based on the registered handler
and the input symbol's shape.
@end itemize

@node Building
@chapter Building

@section Configure Options

BFD, GDB and binutils all read the same @option{--with-msvc-demangler}
flag passed to the top-level @command{configure}.  The top-level
resolves the value once and forwards it explicitly (as @code{yes} or
@code{no}) to every subdirectory.

@table @option
@item --with-msvc-demangler
@itemx --with-msvc-demangler=yes
Enable MSVC demangling.

@item --with-msvc-demangler=no
@itemx --without-msvc-demangler
Disable MSVC demangling.  @file{libdemangle-msvc} is dropped from
@code{configdirs} so it is not built.

@item @r{(omitted)}
Default.  Enabled when a Windows/PE target is configured
(@code{*-*-mingw*}, @code{*-*-cygwin*}, @code{*-*-windows*},
@code{*-*-pe} / @code{*-*-pe-*}, or @option{--enable-targets=all});
disabled otherwise.
@end table

When enabled, each subdirectory's @command{configure} defines
@code{HAVE_MSVC_DEMANGLER} in its own @file{config.h}.  Libiberty
itself has no configure option: the MSVC entry in
@code{libiberty_demanglers[]} is always present, and the dispatch
simply becomes a no-op when no handler is registered.

@section Standalone Build

@example
cd libdemangle-msvc
make clean
make
# Creates libdemangle-msvc.la
@end example

@section Debugging

Pass @option{--enable-msvc-demangle-debug} to @command{configure} to
enable diagnostic output from the msvc demangler's.

@example
./configure --enable-msvc-demangle-debug
@end example

@section ROCgdb Build

@example
./configure --enable-targets=all --with-msvc-demangler
make all-libdemangle-msvc
@end example

@node Testing
@chapter Testing

Run the test suite:

@example
cd libdemangle-msvc/testsuite
make check
@end example

